Positive Matrix Factorization (PMF) was developed by Paatero and Taaper43 and Paatero44. PMF provides a flexible modeling approach that effectively use the concentration and uncertainty data for source profiling. Briefly, PMF decomposes the data matrix X into two sub-data matrices, namely f (p × m) and g (n × p) where f is the specie profile of each source and g describes the contribution characteristic of each source. p is the number of factors extracted. Mathematically, PMF is expressed as Norris et al.45:\documentclass[12pt]{minimal}
				\usepackage{amsmath}
				\usepackage{wasysym} 
				\usepackage{amsfonts} 
				\usepackage{amssymb} 
				\usepackage{amsbsy}
				\usepackage{mathrsfs}
				\usepackage{upgreek}
				\setlength{\oddsidemargin}{-69pt}
				\begin{document}$$x_{ij} = \sum\limits_{k = 1}^{p} {g_{ik} } f_{jk} + e_{ij}$$\end{document}where eij is the residual for each sample species. The unique feature of PMF is the constrained of all the elements of g and f to non-negativity46. This feature allows the analyst to adjust the influence of each data point, depending on the confidence in the measurement. The goal of PMF is to minimize the function Q by iterative-least square method:\documentclass[12pt]{minimal}
