				\begin{document}$$Q = \sum\limits_{i = 1}^{n} {\sum\limits_{j = 1}^{m} {\left[ {\frac{{{\text{ e}}_{{{\mathrm{ij}}}} }}{{u_{ij} }}} \right]} }^{2}$$\end{document}

The elemental values of tested natural radionuclides and radio-elemental ratios in samples from all investigated land uses are presented in Table 10. The average values of eK (in %) ranged from 0.66 (in W&FS) to 1.01 (in DCS). eK (in %) value in DCS was > 1.00 value while the corresponding values in other land uses were < 1.00. Furthermore, eK values in all the studied land uses were lower than the upper crustal abundance value of 2.32% according to UNSCEAR48. The average values of eRa and eTh ranged from 4.67 (in MVS) to 12.69 (in ABT); and 32.43 (in BMS) to 49.76 ppm (in GLS), respectively. The average values of eRa and eTh in soil samples from each land use were clearly above the crustal abundances of 2.78 and 10.77 ppm, respectively, suggesting reworking and human activities in selected land use patterns. The mean values of eRa/eTh in soils from selected land uses ranged from 0.16 (in MVS) to 0.29 (in both ABT and DCS) and all less than 0.33, an indication of non-enrichment of radium in the soil samples of investigated land uses. Furthermore, average eRa/eTh values < 1.25 in all studied land uses suggests that the soil samples were deposited in an oxic-environment71. The eTh/eRa ratios ranged from 3.59 (in ABT) to 13.65 (in MVS). The mean eTh/eRa in all land uses except ABT surpassed the average upper continental crust value of 3.8071,72. This is a further indication of relatively enrichment of thorium and depletion of uranium in collected soil samples. According to71,73, values of eTh/eRa < 2.00 imply presence of reducing environmental conditions; ratio in the range of 2.00–7.00 suggest poor weathering and quick deposition of igneous rock debris while ratios > 7.00 indicates elimination of encouraging uranium enrichment (possibly due to leaching).Table 10
Mean values of Elemental concentration and Radioelemental ratios in selected land use types.
